WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.129 --> 00:00:07.430
Task Force X did its job and made the world
safe…for now.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:11.710
Suicide Squad may be a critical punching bag,
but it still has its rewards for comic book

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:16.110
diehards—including a bunch of references
and homages for the faithful to enjoy.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:21.190
So let's pick up Harley Quinn's mallet and
start smashing some Suicide Squad Easter eggs.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:22.779
Watching The Watchmen

00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:26.659
Remember when Deadshot looks into the clothing
store window and starts moping out about his

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:27.659
daughter?

00:00:27.659 --> 00:00:30.599
That huge yellow smiley face behind the mannequins
wasn't there by chance.

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:35.330
In DC Comics' 2016 Rebirth event, the world
of the Watchmen officially entered the DC

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:36.330
Universe.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.520
And it may only be a matter of time before
that happens in the movies as well, with this

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.000
and similar Easter eggs like the Watchmen
graffiti in Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.300
helping to set up in the inevitable.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:47.670
Frosty Flunky

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:52.330
While the Joker mostly employs nameless goons,
one of his men sports a name tag reading "Mr.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:53.330
Frost."

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:57.500
Brian Azzarello's graphic novel Joker was
told from the perspective of Jonny Frost,

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.990
who goes from being Joker's chauffeur to becoming
his top man.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:04.680
The scenes of Harley shaking her stuff in
a nightclub and Mr. Frost being the Joker's

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.520
number one guy are likely both inspired by
Azzarello's story.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.240
High-Rise Tribute

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.200
This blink-and-you'll-miss-it Easter egg is
pretty easy to figure out if you know your

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.049
DC history: the giant building the team infiltrates
to rescue Amanda Waller is named after comics

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:19.659
writer John Ostrander.

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:23.840
While Ostrander didn't create the original
team that debuted in 1959, he did come up

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.890
with the modern Suicide Squad in 1987.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:28.789
What Goes Around, Comes Around

00:01:28.789 --> 00:01:32.959
When Captain Boomerang convinces Slipknot
his neck-bomb is a dud, the scene mirrors

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:36.969
what happens in the comics after Boomerang
tells Slipknot that their wrist-mounted bombs

00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:37.969
were fake.

00:01:37.969 --> 00:01:42.130
Of course, in the comics, the bomb only blew
off his arm...instead of his head.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.119
Eh you win some, you lose some.

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:46.779
Dancing With The Devil In The Pale Moonlight

00:01:46.779 --> 00:01:50.990
Some people might prefer Harley Quinn's classic
black-and-red costume, but at least her original

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:52.720
outfit appears in the movie.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.639
When the squad prepares to go to Midway City,
Harley pulls out a familiar-looking outfit

00:01:56.639 --> 00:01:59.810
but tosses it aside in favor of something
a bit more revealing.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.889
"We're bad guys, it's what we do."

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:06.899
We also got to see Margot Robbie don the classic
Harley getup in a flashback sequence, which

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:12.640
recreates Alex Ross's iconic cover to 1999's
Batman: Harley Quinn.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.460
Time Away From Mr. J

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.290
After being let down by the Joker a few times
throughout the movie, Harley appears to have

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:21.100
some second thoughts about her maniacal boyfriend.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.260
"Huh?"

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.390
Her budding friendship and flirtatious chemistry
with Will Smith's Deadshot is a callback to

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:30.610
what happens in the comics—and the animated
feature Batman: Assault on Arkham, where things

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:35.600
occasionally got hot, heavy, and downright
weird between these two Batman villains:

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.320
"I got an itch I thought you could help me
scratch Cowboy."

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.000
Flashback

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.570
This Easter egg is kind of obvious, but it's
still pretty awesome.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:49.480
Captain Boomerang's introductory flashback
sequence shows a jewelry store robbery, in

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.200
which his getaway is stopped by Ezra Miller’s
Flash.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.570
It's only right that Captain Boomerang is
thwarted by the Scarlet Speedster, since he

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.730
first appeared as a Flash villain way back
in 1960.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.370
In an interview with Collider, director David
Ayer revealed that this scene was shot while

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:04.410
Suicide Squad was in post-production.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:08.340
The scene was directed by Batman v. Superman
director, Zack Snyder while he was filming

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:09.500
Justice League.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.500
Thanks for watching!

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.210
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:17.030
And leave us a comment to let us know if there
are any other Suicide Squad Easter eggs that

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:17.959
should've made the list...

